<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<aiml version="2.0">

    <!--
      Additional generic/domain-adjacent training. This file is intentionally
      additive: it does not rewrite existing X-Men AIML data.
    -->

    <category>
        <pattern>ANSWER SMARTLY</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[A smart answer should do three things: name the real issue, explain the trade-off, and suggest the next action. For X-Men, that usually means connecting the user's question to one of four things: the protocol step, the mutation type, the derivation mode, or the generated evidence.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] How should I think about this? | Which mutation should I pick? | How do I read the generated output?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>GIVE ME A SMART ANSWER</pattern><template><srai>ANSWER SMARTLY</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>BE SMART</pattern><template><srai>ANSWER SMARTLY</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>SOUND SMART</pattern><template><srai>ANSWER SMARTLY</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>HOW SHOULD I THINK ABOUT THIS</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Start by asking what kind of mistake you want to model.

- If a step might disappear, use a Skip mutation.
- If a message might be malformed, use Replace or Add.
- If a participant may lose knowledge, use Forget.
- If a participant may ignore an available fact, use Neglect.

That framing is better than choosing mutations by name because it keeps the analysis tied to the behaviour you are testing.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] Which mutation should I pick? | What is the difference between Forget and Neglect? | What mutations are available?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>HOW DO I THINK ABOUT THIS</pattern><template><srai>HOW SHOULD I THINK ABOUT THIS</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>HOW SHOULD I APPROACH THIS</pattern><template><srai>HOW SHOULD I THINK ABOUT THIS</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT IS THE RIGHT WAY TO THINK ABOUT THIS</pattern><template><srai>HOW SHOULD I THINK ABOUT THIS</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>CAN YOU ANALYSE THIS</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Yes, but I need the analysis target. Tell me one of these: the mutation you selected, the protocol behaviour you are suspicious of, the error message you saw, or the part of the generated output that looks wrong.

Once I have that, I can reason about whether the issue is selection, vocabulary/profile matching, derivation depth, or the mutation engine itself.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] Which mutation should I pick? | How do I debug no mutations generated? | How does the analysis pipeline work?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>ANALYSE THIS</pattern><template><srai>CAN YOU ANALYSE THIS</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>CAN YOU ANALYZE THIS</pattern><template><srai>CAN YOU ANALYSE THIS</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>ANALYZE THIS</pattern><template><srai>CAN YOU ANALYSE THIS</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS FORMAL VERIFICATION</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Formal verification is the practice of checking a system against a precise mathematical model instead of relying only on examples or manual testing. In protocol work, that matters because a single missing assumption can create a security claim that looks true in normal testing but fails under an adversarial trace.

X-Men sits around that process: it generates altered protocol models so Tamarin can test whether the original security argument survives plausible human or ceremony mistakes.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Tamarin? | How does the analysis pipeline work? | Why use mutation testing?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>EXPLAIN FORMAL VERIFICATION</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS FORMAL VERIFICATION</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT ARE FORMAL METHODS</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS FORMAL VERIFICATION</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>EXPLAIN FORMAL METHODS</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS FORMAL VERIFICATION</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS TAMARIN</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Tamarin is a protocol-verification tool. You describe a protocol as rules and facts, then ask whether security properties hold across the traces the model can produce.

For this app, the important point is simple: X-Men creates mutated Tamarin models, and Tamarin checks whether those altered models still satisfy the properties you care about.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] How does the analysis pipeline work? | What is a SPTHY file? | Why use mutation testing?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>EXPLAIN TAMARIN</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS TAMARIN</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT IS TAMARIN PROVER</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS TAMARIN</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS A SPTHY FILE</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[A .spthy file is a Tamarin theory file. It usually contains the protocol rules, facts, functions, equations, and lemmas that define what the protocol can do and what properties should hold.

In X-Men, uploading a .spthy file gives the mutation engine the model it needs to create altered variants for analysis.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] How does the analysis pipeline work? | What mutations are available? | How do I debug no mutations generated?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>WHAT IS SPTHY</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS A SPTHY FILE</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>EXPLAIN SPTHY</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS A SPTHY FILE</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT IS A TAMARIN FILE</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS A SPTHY FILE</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHY USE MUTATION TESTING</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Mutation testing is useful because it asks a sharper question than "does the original model verify?" It asks whether the model still behaves safely after a controlled mistake is introduced.

For ceremony analysis, that is valuable because users can skip, forget, mistype, or ignore steps. A mutation turns that human possibility into a concrete model that Tamarin can check.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] What mutations are available? | Which mutation should I pick? | What is the difference between Skip and Forget?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>WHAT IS MUTATION TESTING</pattern><template><srai>WHY USE MUTATION TESTING</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>EXPLAIN MUTATION TESTING</pattern><template><srai>WHY USE MUTATION TESTING</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHY MUTATE A PROTOCOL</pattern><template><srai>WHY USE MUTATION TESTING</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS HUMAN ERROR</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Human error, in this app, means a behaviour that a real participant might plausibly perform even when the protocol assumes perfect execution. The point is not to blame the user. The point is to test whether the ceremony is robust when attention, memory, typing, or interpretation is imperfect.

That is why X-Men has mutations such as Skip, Forget, Replace, Add, and Neglect: each one models a different kind of human deviation.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] Which mutation should I pick? | What is Forget? | What is Neglect?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>EXPLAIN HUMAN ERROR</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS HUMAN ERROR</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT IS CEREMONY ERROR</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS HUMAN ERROR</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN SKIP AND FORGET</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Skip removes an action from the trace. Forget removes or weakens remembered knowledge.

So the distinction is behavioural: Skip asks "what if this event never happens?" Forget asks "what if the participant no longer has the information needed at this point?" They can both expose fragile assumptions, but they stress different parts of the ceremony.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Skip? | What is Forget? | Which mutation should I pick?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>COMPARE SKIP AND FORGET</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN SKIP AND FORGET</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>SKIP VS FORGET</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN SKIP AND FORGET</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN FORGET AND NEGLECT</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Forget is about losing knowledge. Neglect is about failing to use knowledge that is still available.

That difference matters. Forget models memory or state loss. Neglect models attention, omission, or a participant choosing the wrong usable fact. If the question is "did they still know it?", use Forget. If the question is "did they use it?", use Neglect.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Forget? | What is Neglect? | Which mutation should I pick?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>COMPARE FORGET AND NEGLECT</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN FORGET AND NEGLECT</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>FORGET VS NEGLECT</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN FORGET AND NEGLECT</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>HOW DO I DEBUG NO MUTATIONS GENERATED</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Check it in this order:

- Confirm a .spthy file is uploaded.
- Confirm at least one mutation is selected.
- Confirm the active vocabulary profile matches the uploaded model.
- For Forget, confirm the required function or derivation input exists.
- If the response is empty, try a simpler mutation first, such as Send or Receive, to separate UI/setup issues from deeper derivation issues.

That sequence avoids chasing the hard case before the basic inputs are known-good.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] What mutations are available? | What is a vocabulary profile? | Which mutation should I pick?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>NO MUTATIONS GENERATED</pattern><template><srai>HOW DO I DEBUG NO MUTATIONS GENERATED</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHY DID NO FILE GENERATE</pattern><template><srai>HOW DO I DEBUG NO MUTATIONS GENERATED</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHY NO FILE GENERATED</pattern><template><srai>HOW DO I DEBUG NO MUTATIONS GENERATED</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS A VOCABULARY PROFILE</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[A vocabulary profile tells X-Men which protocol terms represent actions, values, and domain-specific concepts in a model. Without that mapping, the mutation engine may not know which facts are send events, receive events, setup facts, or typed values.

The practical rule is: if the uploaded file uses a different vocabulary, the profile must match it before mutation generation can be reliable.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] How does the analysis pipeline work? | How do I debug no mutations generated? | What case studies do you support?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>EXPLAIN VOCABULARY PROFILE</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS A VOCABULARY PROFILE</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT ARE PROFILES</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS A VOCABULARY PROFILE</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>HOW DO I READ THE GENERATED OUTPUT</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Read the generated output as a question, not as a final verdict. Each mutated file asks: if this specific human deviation happens, do the security properties still hold?

The useful reading order is: identify the mutation, find the changed rule or fact, then compare the verification result with the original model. A failure is interesting because it points to a protocol assumption that may need explanation or redesign.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] Why use mutation testing? | How does the analysis pipeline work? | Which mutation should I pick?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>HOW TO READ OUTPUT</pattern><template><srai>HOW DO I READ THE GENERATED OUTPUT</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>HOW DO I READ MUTATED FILES</pattern><template><srai>HOW DO I READ THE GENERATED OUTPUT</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT DOES THE OUTPUT MEAN</pattern><template><srai>HOW DO I READ THE GENERATED OUTPUT</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>EXPLAIN THIS SIMPLY</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[The simple version is this: X-Men makes controlled "what if the human makes this mistake?" versions of a protocol model. Tamarin then checks those versions. If a security property breaks, the mutation has found a useful stress point.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] Why use mutation testing? | Which mutation should I pick? | How does the analysis pipeline work?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>EXPLAIN IN SIMPLE TERMS</pattern><template><srai>EXPLAIN THIS SIMPLY</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>EXPLAIN LIKE I AM NEW</pattern><template><srai>EXPLAIN THIS SIMPLY</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT SHOULD I DO NEXT</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[If you are at the start, upload the .spthy file and select one mutation that matches the behaviour you want to test. If you already generated files, inspect one mutated file first before running a broad batch. Broad mutation sets are useful, but one well-understood mutation usually teaches more than twenty unchecked outputs.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] Which mutation should I pick? | How do I read the generated output? | How do I debug no mutations generated?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>NEXT STEP</pattern><template><srai>WHAT SHOULD I DO NEXT</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT NEXT</pattern><template><srai>WHAT SHOULD I DO NEXT</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHEN YOU ARE NOT SURE</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[When I am not sure, I should not invent certainty. I should say what is missing, offer the closest useful directions, and give you choices that move the work forward.

For this assistant, that means I will usually steer you toward mutation selection, vocabulary/profile matching, derivation settings, or output interpretation.
[[FOLLOWUPS]] What can you help me with? | Which mutation should I pick? | How do I debug no mutations generated?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU DO NOT KNOW</pattern><template><srai>WHEN YOU ARE NOT SURE</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT IF YOU DO NOT KNOW</pattern><template><srai>WHEN YOU ARE NOT SURE</srai></template></category>

</aiml>
